'' 'f( args ) = value' IS NOT AN ASSIGNMENT, and tiko-lang treated every one as if it
'' were -- which declined where the result was BYVAL, because there is no address to
'' store through, and silently stored through the reference where it was byref.
''
'' fbc warns 34 -- "'=' parsed as equality operator in function argument, not
'' assignment to BYREF function result" -- and re-reads the statement as the CALL
'' 'f( args = value )', passing the comparison. Measured, 'f1( 123 ) = 123' is
'' '_Z2F1u7INTEGER( -1ll )': 123 = 123 is true, which is -1.
''
'' THE WARNING'S OWN WORDING SUGGESTS THE RULE IS ABOUT BYREF, AND IT IS NOT. Measured
'' by running all four shapes:
''
''   br( 1 ) = 456     BYREF result, one argument     prints 0    -- re-read
''   br0( ) = 456      BYREF result, no arguments     prints 456  -- assignment
''   a.At( 2 ) = 88    BYREF MEMBER, one argument     prints 0    -- re-read
''   a[ 3 ] = 99       'operator []'                  prints 99   -- assignment
''
'' What decides is that ARGUMENTS WERE WRITTEN. With none there is nothing to fold the
'' '=' into. The BRACKET spelling is a different statement and stays an lvalue; so does
'' an ARRAY subscript, whose callee is a variable rather than a procedure.
''
'' The rewrite happens in Resolve, which is the first pass that knows the callee is a
'' PROCEDURE and not an array. It is the only place there that builds a node, and it is
'' spelled out rather than borrowed for that reason.
'' TEST: cli
'' ARGS: --dump-ir tests/mir/src/call-assign-reread.bas
'' EXPECT-EXIT: 0
'' EXPECT-OUT: verify: ok
'' EXPECT-OUT: 0 statements not lowered
'' EXPECT-OUT: icmp.eq
'' GOLDEN: expected/call-assign-reread.out
